WEBVTT

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:18.600
Do you hear?  Hooves beating

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:30.750
A carriage goes through the gentle hills

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:37.980
Someone comes to pick me up

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:45.950
And takes me

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:49.900
To the avenue with white flowers

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:58.050
To the home of wind

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:02.400
He takes me

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.900
I'm sure

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:33.020
Everyone, I have big news!

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:35.170
Oh, it's Ruby!

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:37.770
What happened, Ruby?

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:42.430
I'm tired.

00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:45.940
What happened? What big news?

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:51.690
Mr. Phillips is going to resign, in June.

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:54.330
Is that true, Ruby?

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:55.690
My, I'm so happy!

00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:58.640
Then, we'll have a new teacher
for the new term?

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:00.630
What kind of teacher will we have?

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:03.050
It would be nice if it was a female teacher.

00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:05.730
Oh, I hope it's a male teacher.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:08.400
And hopefully he's tall and handsome.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:10.450
Ruby, you're really...

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:16.150
But why is Mr. Phillips' resigning?

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:23.000
Because Prissy is going to Queen's Academy,
so he's moving to Charlottetown.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:26.280
I heard their conversation.

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:30.980
Then we'll never see that
fantastic moustache again.

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:32.950
I feel quite sad.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:35.360
How could you?

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:41.650
I don't feel a bit sad for
Mr. Phillips' resignation.

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:43.180
It's the opposite.

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:47.830
That's right. I won't cry when he leaves.

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:49.940
Me neither.

00:02:50.710 --> 00:02:53.560
I won't shed a single tear.

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:55.870
It's a pity, but me neither.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:57.400
Me neither.

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:02.760
You're right. I'm sure I won't cry.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:04.200
Of course you won't.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.960
You were the one who suffered the most.

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:15.650
Then...

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:19.410
Anne Shirley.

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:24.210
You must be anne Shirley
from the orphan asylum?

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:30.370
To Anne, Mr. Phillips never gave her a
good impression from the start.

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:38.760
That's why she, like everyone else,
felt happy about Mr. Phillips resignation.

00:03:39.710 --> 00:03:48.840
And when Ruby Gillis showed her new
dress, eveyone forgot about their teacher.

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:53.980
Ruby's clothing had puffed
sleeves like anne loved

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:57.580
and was the latest brand of fashion.

00:03:58.190 --> 00:04:02.030
Then the last day of June came.

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:10.790
Really, what is it this time?

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:15.960
I'm home, Marilla.

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:04.970
Dear me, there is nothing but meetings
and partings in this world, as Mrs. Lynde says.

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:13.240
Wasn't it fortunate, Marilla, that I took
an extra handkerchief to school today?

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:16.660
I had a presentiment that
it would be needed.

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:19.520
I never thought you
were so fond of Mr. Phillips

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:24.450
that you'd require two
handkerchiefs to dry your tears

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:26.340
just because he was going away.

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:30.770
I don't think I was crying because
I was really so very fond of him.

00:05:30.850 --> 00:05:32.880
My, my. Then why did you?

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:35.020
I just cried because all the others did.

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:37.790
It was Ruby Gillis started it.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:42.090
Ruby has always
declared she hated Mr. Phillips,

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:45.070
but just as soon as he
got up to make his farewell speech

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:46.520
she burst into tears.

00:05:54.590 --> 00:05:56.910
The time has come for us...

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:01.220
Look forward!

00:06:03.710 --> 00:06:07.430
Your inability to listen to classes
has not changed a bit, Roman Campbell!

00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:10.470
When the new teacher comes,
you shouldn't be like that.

00:06:13.750 --> 00:06:14.810
Then...

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:18.080
The time has come for us to part.

00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:24.260
There were many heavy memories,
like the times I told you to stay quiet,

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:29.130
in this period of time I spent
with all of you, that I shall never forget.

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:34.620
Some of you feel hurt, and some dislike me.

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:44.590
But I've tried my best to tutor you
in all of these three years.

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:46.860
Although I would use
the rod to discipline at times.

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:49.650
But that was the rod of love.

00:06:49.790 --> 00:06:55.070
I believe you would understand me one day.

00:06:56.210 --> 00:07:00.180
That's right! The time has come for us to part.

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:06.120
Everyone worked hard for these 3 years.

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:18.890
Everyone before me has grown up so much
since I came here 3 years ago in June.

00:07:18.890 --> 00:07:21.200
When I think of that, I...

00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:23.410
I...

00:07:30.590 --> 00:07:34.380
Even if we should part,

00:07:34.660 --> 00:07:38.020
in my heart there will be memories
of the time we spent together.

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:39.900
No matter happy or sad.

00:07:40.510 --> 00:07:50.180
The time we worked hard together
shall be firmly imprinted upon my mind.

00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:53.450
Fare thee well. Like Shakespeare said,

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:56.790
"The elements be kind to thee, and make
Thy spirits all of comfort."

00:07:57.070 --> 00:08:03.070
I shall leave as the dust.
Parting... with...

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:05.780
I was so firm I wouldn't cry, Marilla.

00:08:06.170 --> 00:08:09.820
Then all the girls began to cry,
one after the other.

00:08:09.910 --> 00:08:14.240
I didn't want to cry for him
and tried to hold out.

00:08:14.790 --> 00:08:17.680
I tried to remember the time Mr. Phillips
made me sit with Gil...

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:21.860
...I tried to remember the
time he made me sit with a, boy.

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:29.490
And since you seem to be so
fond of the boys company,

00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:33.210
go and sit with Gilbert Blythe.

00:08:33.700 --> 00:08:39.840
And the time he spelled my name
without an e on the blackboard

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:42.860
and how he said I was the worst dunce
he ever saw at geometry

00:08:43.490 --> 00:08:46.210
and laughed at my spelling.

00:08:47.510 --> 00:08:51.020
And all the times he
had been so horrid and sarcastic.

00:08:52.550 --> 00:08:57.160
But somehow I couldn't, Marilla,

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:00.200
and I just had to cry too.

00:09:01.180 --> 00:09:06.820
Jane Andrews has been talking about how
glad she'd be when Mr. Phillips went away

00:09:06.820 --> 00:09:10.780
and she declared she'd never shed a tear.

00:09:10.780 --> 00:09:16.890
Well, she was worse than any of us and had
to borrow a handkerchief from her brother.

00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:20.040
Of course the boys didn't cry.

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:24.650
Jane hadn't brought one of her own,
not expecting to need it.

00:09:24.650 --> 00:09:28.630
Oh, Marilla, it was heartrending.

00:09:30.410 --> 00:09:35.560
Mr. Phillips, had tears in his eyes too.

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:38.940
I felt dreadfully sorry and remorseful

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:42.590
for all the times I'd talked in school

00:09:42.590 --> 00:09:45.250
and drawn pictures of him on my slate

00:09:45.250 --> 00:09:48.490
and made fun of him and Prissy.

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:54.150
I can tell you I wished I'd been a
model pupil like Minnie Andrews.

00:09:54.150 --> 00:09:57.790
She hadn't anything on her conscience.

00:09:57.790 --> 00:10:01.870
Well, it's too late for regrets now.

00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:04.420
You're right.

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:21.920
After that, the girls cried all
the way home from school.

00:10:30.730 --> 00:10:33.640
Ruby Gillis kept saying every few minutes...

00:10:33.730 --> 00:10:35.960
The time has come for us to part!

00:10:35.960 --> 00:10:37.710
Moving, isn't it?

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:42.690
And that would start us off again whenever
we were in any danger of cheering up.

00:10:47.750 --> 00:10:50.380
The time has come for us to part!

00:10:51.020 --> 00:10:56.360
Ruby kept repeating it, making our eyes all red.

00:10:56.600 --> 00:11:00.320
And Ruby was the one who wailed the loudest.

00:11:19.630 --> 00:11:23.370
Don't tease me anymore, Anne.
I can't work if this goes on.

00:11:23.660 --> 00:11:26.270
Oh, I do feel dreadfully sad.

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:31.130
But, Marilla, one can't
feel quite in the depths of despair

00:11:31.300 --> 00:11:35.820
with two months' vacation
before them, can they?

00:11:35.940 --> 00:11:42.090
And besides, we met the new minister and
his wife coming from the station.

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:44.880
My, I didn't know they were coming today.

00:11:45.300 --> 00:11:48.500
It was when we cried ourselves
silly and were at the road.

00:11:49.210 --> 00:11:50.910
Look. It's Mr. Bell's carriage.

00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:06.890
Good afternoon, Mr. Bell.

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:08.520
Good afternoon.

00:12:09.730 --> 00:12:11.430
His wife is very pretty.

00:12:11.430 --> 00:12:14.600
Not exactly regally lovely, of course.

00:12:14.860 --> 00:12:20.140
It wouldn't do, I suppose, for a
minister to have a regally lovely wife.

00:12:20.230 --> 00:12:21.580
Of course.

00:12:22.520 --> 00:12:28.000
She was dressed in blue muslin with
lovely puffed sleeves

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:31.080
and a hat trimmed with roses.

00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:40.560
Jane Andrews said she thought puffed sleeves
were too worldly for a minister's wife,

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:43.140
but I didn't make any such
uncharitable remark,

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:47.500
because I know what it is
to long for puffed sleeves.

00:12:47.630 --> 00:12:53.340
Then are they going to board with
Rachel, until the manse is ready?

00:12:53.340 --> 00:12:57.690
It appears so, because Mrs. Lynde
was on the carriage too.

00:13:03.860 --> 00:13:07.630
That night, Marilla went to
Mrs. Rachel Lynde place

00:13:07.630 --> 00:13:13.310
to return the quilting frames she had
borrowed the preceding winter.

00:13:16.040 --> 00:13:19.760
Many a thing Mrs. Lynde had lent,

00:13:20.020 --> 00:13:23.920
sometimes never expecting to see it again,

00:13:24.310 --> 00:13:29.930
came home that night in charge
of the borrowers thereof.

00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:33.510
A new minister,

00:13:33.510 --> 00:13:36.330
and moreover a minister with a wife,

00:13:36.330 --> 00:13:40.770
was a lawful object of curiosity in a
quiet little country settlement

00:13:40.770 --> 00:13:44.420
where sensations were few and far between.

00:13:44.690 --> 00:13:49.950
Everyone tried to see the new minister
and his wife, but they didn't appear.

00:13:50.460 --> 00:13:54.770
This made Marilla and villagers
very disappointed.

00:14:05.080 --> 00:14:07.130
Anne, wait for me!

00:14:08.390 --> 00:14:09.530
Oh!

00:14:11.710 --> 00:14:13.100
What's wrong, Anne?

00:14:16.270 --> 00:14:17.990
It's the minister and his wife!

00:14:31.770 --> 00:14:33.710
It looks like a painting!

00:14:33.710 --> 00:14:36.980
Two lovers walking together on Lover's Lane.

00:14:36.980 --> 00:14:38.390
The minister?

00:14:38.390 --> 00:14:39.240
Let's go!

00:14:40.030 --> 00:14:41.100
let's follow them!

00:14:41.100 --> 00:14:42.650
Anne Shirley!

00:15:15.240 --> 00:15:16.100
How wonderful.

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:18.280
But quite unlike a minister.

00:15:18.280 --> 00:15:19.450
That's true.

00:15:19.450 --> 00:15:21.200
But it's still wonderful.

00:15:29.670 --> 00:15:30.950
They're coming over here!

00:15:30.950 --> 00:15:31.840
What should we do?

00:15:31.840 --> 00:15:33.410
Let's greet them.

00:15:38.340 --> 00:15:41.940
Good afternoon.

00:15:43.010 --> 00:15:44.560
Are you from around here?

00:15:44.560 --> 00:15:45.870
Yes, we are.

00:15:45.870 --> 00:15:47.680
Thank goodness!

00:15:48.770 --> 00:15:51.320
Could you tell us where does
this road leads to?

00:15:51.370 --> 00:15:54.710
We got lost while taking a walk.

00:15:54.880 --> 00:15:57.740
This road is called the Lover's Lane.

00:16:01.210 --> 00:16:03.370
I'm Anne Shirley.

00:16:03.540 --> 00:16:06.340
I live in Green Gables.

00:16:06.410 --> 00:16:08.680
This is Diana Barry.

00:16:08.680 --> 00:16:10.590
I Live in Orchard Slope.

00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:13.290
You're Miss Barry from
the house by Barry's pond?

00:16:13.310 --> 00:16:17.430
Yes. But we call that
the Lake of Shining Waters.

00:16:17.520 --> 00:16:20.660
Oh! What a wonderful name.

00:16:20.780 --> 00:16:22.080
It was Anne that thought of it.

00:16:22.290 --> 00:16:24.240
Anne is very good at naming things.

00:16:31.840 --> 00:16:33.270
It's really a good name.

00:16:34.400 --> 00:16:35.300
Well, we are...

00:16:35.300 --> 00:16:36.190
We know.

00:16:36.190 --> 00:16:39.660
You're the minister and his wife,
Mr. and Mrs. Allan, isn't that right?

00:16:39.870 --> 00:16:44.010
Well, well.
Looks like we've become famous.

00:16:44.390 --> 00:16:48.210
When we were returning from school
yesterday, we saw you in the carriage.

00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:53.180
If you want to return to Mrs. Lynde's
house, we can lead the way.

00:16:53.580 --> 00:16:54.630
Thank you very much.

00:16:55.270 --> 00:16:57.580
Let's let them show the way.

00:16:58.700 --> 00:16:59.790
Please do that.

00:17:00.190 --> 00:17:00.880
Yes.

00:17:28.490 --> 00:17:31.090
The scenery here is wonderful.

00:17:31.200 --> 00:17:32.200
A forest,

00:17:32.200 --> 00:17:32.970
a brook

00:17:33.290 --> 00:17:34.580
and a lake.

00:17:36.290 --> 00:17:38.160
Are you thirsty?

00:17:38.560 --> 00:17:40.750
The Dryad's Bubble is just around here.

00:17:41.800 --> 00:17:43.580
The Dryad's Bubble, is it?

00:17:43.910 --> 00:17:45.930
You thought of that name yourself?

00:17:46.150 --> 00:17:46.930
Yes.

00:17:46.930 --> 00:17:47.700
This way.

00:18:06.430 --> 00:18:09.490
I met another kindred spirit.

00:18:10.120 --> 00:18:13.930
We led them to Mrs. Lynde's house.

00:18:15.190 --> 00:18:17.050
She was a splendid missus.

00:18:17.330 --> 00:18:20.540
Composed and gentle, reasonable even.

00:18:21.300 --> 00:18:24.520
And she'll head our Sunday classes.

00:18:24.930 --> 00:18:26.870
This is such a good coincidence.

00:18:27.740 --> 00:18:31.700
From now on, I'll really won't
miss Sunday classes.

00:18:31.700 --> 00:18:33.530
Even if it rains.

00:18:33.530 --> 00:18:36.680
Oh! Why doesn't Sunday come quickly?

00:18:36.680 --> 00:18:42.790
Goodness, I never dreamed you would
look forward to Sunday classes.

00:18:43.050 --> 00:18:48.400
We saw them dancing waltz.

00:18:49.530 --> 00:18:51.730
What? Dancing waltz?

00:18:51.900 --> 00:18:54.750
That may be troublesome for a minister.

00:18:54.790 --> 00:18:58.190
I've never heard of a minister dancing before.

00:18:58.190 --> 00:19:03.060
Oh! Just a little dance is not bad, is it?

00:19:03.640 --> 00:19:05.600
It was really wonderful.

00:19:06.460 --> 00:19:09.500
They must be in deep love.

00:19:11.890 --> 00:19:15.060
I was a little surprised when I saw it!

00:19:15.060 --> 00:19:21.380
But the weather was great and the
scenery was beautiful, so they danced.

00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:26.650
The minister didn't think it
was wrong. I'm glad!

00:19:28.800 --> 00:19:31.150
On the next Sunday,

00:19:31.150 --> 00:19:36.160
Marilla's head throbbed but
she still went to the church.

00:19:38.020 --> 00:19:42.720
And of course, everyone of Avonlea was there.

00:19:42.720 --> 00:19:49.320
Besides the sick people, only
Matthew Cuthbert wasn't there.

00:19:51.860 --> 00:19:58.590
Before the seats filed out, Mr. Allan
delivered a heartfelt and touching sermon.

00:19:59.000 --> 00:20:04.820
And all were touched by the
emotions of this young minister.

00:20:07.410 --> 00:20:10.380
And on Sunday School,

00:20:10.450 --> 00:20:17.610
Mrs. Allan taught some happy
songs and earned the children's heart.

00:20:18.410 --> 00:20:24.820
Mrs. Allan's teachings were different
from the melancholy of previous lessons.

00:20:27.750 --> 00:20:32.270
And on the way home, Marilla said...

00:20:33.590 --> 00:20:37.030
They aren't as strange
as you describe them to be.

00:20:37.150 --> 00:20:40.040
Oh! I never said they were strange.

00:20:40.180 --> 00:20:42.000
I only said they were wonderful.

00:20:42.120 --> 00:20:46.770
The wonderful people
you talk about are mostly strange.

00:20:48.650 --> 00:20:52.250
But I'm very glad they've called Mr. Allan.

00:20:52.710 --> 00:20:56.520
His sermon was interesting
and he prayed as if he meant it

00:20:56.520 --> 00:20:59.100
and not just as if he did it
because he was in the habit of it.

00:20:59.470 --> 00:21:03.630
Mrs. Lynde says he isn't perfect,

00:21:03.870 --> 00:21:06.390
but she says she supposes we couldn't

00:21:06.390 --> 00:21:09.650
expect a perfect minister for
seven hundred and fifty dollars a year.

00:21:11.460 --> 00:21:15.540
Mrs. Allan is perfectly lovely,
and she's a splendid teacher.

00:21:15.900 --> 00:21:20.320
She said right away she didn't think it was
fair for the teacher to ask all the questions.

00:21:21.200 --> 00:21:24.300
And you know, that is exactly
what I've always thought.

00:21:25.080 --> 00:21:27.020
Everyone was surprised.

00:21:27.700 --> 00:21:29.030
I asked ever so many.

00:21:29.320 --> 00:21:31.520
I'm good at asking questions.

00:21:31.720 --> 00:21:32.990
I believe you.

00:21:33.670 --> 00:21:37.120
Nobody else asked any except Ruby Gillis.

00:21:37.800 --> 00:21:42.740
She asked if there was
to be a Sunday-school picnic this summer.

00:21:43.300 --> 00:21:47.370
I didn't think that was
a very proper question to ask.

00:21:48.100 --> 00:21:52.460
But Mrs. Allan just smiled and
said she thought there would be.

00:21:52.950 --> 00:21:55.330
Mrs. Allan has a lovely smile.

00:21:55.450 --> 00:21:58.580
She has such
EXQUISITE dimples in her cheeks.

00:21:59.060 --> 00:22:02.600
I wish I had
dimples in my cheeks, Marilla.

00:22:04.600 --> 00:22:10.150
I'm not half so skinny as I was when I
came here, but I have no dimples yet.

00:22:16.590 --> 00:22:22.850
If I had perhaps I could influence
people for good.

00:22:23.850 --> 00:22:32.020
Mrs. Allan said we ought always
to try to influence other people for good.

00:22:32.610 --> 00:22:35.990
She talked so nice about everything.

00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:40.320
I never knew before
that religion was such a cheerful thing.

00:22:40.770 --> 00:22:43.290
I always thought it
was kind of melancholy.

00:22:43.480 --> 00:22:45.450
But Mrs. Allan's isn't.

00:22:51.490 --> 00:22:52.490
Listen, Anne.

00:22:52.610 --> 00:22:56.620
I suppose we must have Mr.
and Mrs. Allan up to tea someday soon.

00:22:56.900 --> 00:22:58.880
Oh, Marilla. Really?

00:22:58.930 --> 00:23:04.630
Rachel said they've been
most everywhere but here.

00:23:05.210 --> 00:23:06.250
Let me see.

00:23:07.000 --> 00:23:09.480
Next Wednesday would
be a good time to have them,

00:23:09.820 --> 00:23:12.540
but don't say a
word to Matthew about it.

00:23:12.800 --> 00:23:13.840
Why?

00:23:14.440 --> 00:23:20.940
If he knew they were coming
he'd find some excuse to be away that day.

00:23:22.030 --> 00:23:25.730
He'd got so used to
Mr. Bentley he didn't mind him,

00:23:25.860 --> 00:23:29.440
but he's going to find it hard
to get acquainted with a new minister.

00:23:29.900 --> 00:23:33.310
And a new minister's
wife will frighten him to death.

00:23:36.180 --> 00:23:38.330
I'll be as secret as the dead, Marilla.

00:23:38.610 --> 00:23:42.510
But, will you let me
make a cake for the occasion?

00:23:42.810 --> 00:23:43.770
A cake?

00:23:43.910 --> 00:23:44.570
You?

00:23:44.790 --> 00:23:47.740
Yes. I'd love to
do something for Mrs. Allan.

00:23:48.200 --> 00:23:51.670
And you know I can make
a pretty good cake by this time.

00:23:52.090 --> 00:23:55.060
Let me see...
You can make a layer cake.

00:23:56.270 --> 00:23:57.600
Marilla, thank you!

00:24:05.980 --> 00:24:10.130
Anne fell promptly and
wholeheartedly in love With Mrs. Allan.

00:24:17.860 --> 00:24:24.490
She works hard to prepare for the coming
of the Allan couple to Green Gables.

00:24:24.900 --> 00:24:27.430
Next episode of Anne of Green Gables:

00:24:27.820 --> 00:24:28.830
The Wrong Ingredient

00:24:29.200 --> 00:24:30.160
Please watch!
